  "Number": 20,
  "Question": "For how many positive integers \\(x\\) is \\(\\log_{10}(x - 40)\\) \\(+ \\log_{10}(60 - x) \\lt 2?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(10\\)",
    "B": "\\(18\\)",
    "C": "\\(19\\)",
    "D": "\\(20\\)",
    "E": "infinitely many"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "Both logarithm arguments must be positive, so\n\\[\nx-40>0,\\qquad 60-x>0,\n\\]\nand hence \\(40<x<60\\). On this domain, combine the logarithms. Since the base \\(10\\) logarithm is increasing and \\(2=\\log_{10}100\\), the given inequality is equivalent to\n\\[\n(x-40)(60-x)<100.\n\\]\nThe two factors are equally far from \\(10\\). Setting \\(y=x-50\\) reveals a difference of squares:\n\\[\n(x-40)(60-x)=(10+y)(10-y)=100-y^2.\n\\]\nThus the inequality holds exactly when \\(y^2>0\\), or \\(x\\ne50\\).\n\nThe integers in the domain are \\(41,42,\\ldots,59\\), a total of \\(59-41+1=19\\). Removing \\(50\\), where equality holds, leaves \\(\\boxed{18}\\) solutions, choice B.",
